-- title:
--   Enlarging Lemma, §D.2 (corrected: commutators of the generated algebras, $d\ge3$)
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal H=\mathbb C^d$, let $|i\rangle,|j\rangle$ be linearly independent vectors, and let $\mathcal H_1,\mathcal H_2$ be sets of Hamiltonians (represented by the anti-Hermitian matrices $iH$). Suppose that
--
--   - the real Lie algebra generated by $\mathcal H_1$ contains $\mathfrak{su}(d-1)$ acting on the orthogonal complement $|i\rangle^\perp$ (and as zero on $|i\rangle$), and
--   - the real Lie algebra generated by $\mathcal H_2$ contains $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ acting on $\mathrm{span}\{|i\rangle,|j\rangle\}$ (and as zero on its orthogonal complement).
--
--   Then:
--
--   1. the Lie algebra generated by $\mathcal H_1\cup\mathcal H_2$ contains $\mathfrak{su}(d)$;
--   2. if $d\ge3$, the commutators $[A,B]$ with $A$ in the Lie algebra generated by $\mathcal H_1$ and $B$ in the one generated by $\mathcal H_2$ generate exactly $\mathfrak{su}(d)$ as a Lie algebra.
--
--   The lemma is the last step of the special case $J=n/2-1$ of the proof of Theorem C.2.1.
--
--   Read literally, with commutators of the generating sets only, the book's conclusion is false. Take $d=3$ with orthonormal $|i\rangle,|j\rangle,|k\rangle$, $\mathcal H_1=\{X_{jk},Y_{jk}\}$ and $\mathcal H_2=\{X_{ij},Y_{ij}\}$: these generate $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ on $\mathrm{span}\{|j\rangle,|k\rangle\}$ and on $\mathrm{span}\{|i\rangle,|j\rangle\}$, but their four commutators generate only $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ on $\mathrm{span}\{|i\rangle,|k\rangle\}$. At $d=2$, $\mathfrak{su}(1)=0$ and all commutators vanish. The book's proof itself takes $X_{jk}$ in the algebra generated by $\mathcal H_1$, which is the reading stated here.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathfrak{su}(W)$ extended by zero is `suOn`. Lie algebras are real Lie spans of matrices.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: "[H1, H2] (all commutators) generates su(d) under closure as a Lie-algebra" → for d ≥ 3, the commutators [A, B] with A ∈ Lie(H1), B ∈ Lie(H2) generate exactly su(d); reason: with commutators of the generating sets it is false (d = 3, H1 = {iX_jk, iY_jk}, H2 = {iX_ij, iY_ij}: the four commutators generate a 3-dimensional algebra, su(2) on span{i, k}), and at d = 2 every commutator is 0 since su(1) = 0
--   - generalization: "H1 generates su(d − 1)", "H2 generates su(2)" (equalities) → the generated Lie algebras contain these; reason: weaker hypotheses with the same conclusion; specializes to the equality case
--   - generalization: the conclusion about [H1, H2] alone → also clause 1, Lie(H1 ∪ H2) ⊇ su(d), for every d; reason: it is the form in which Appendix C uses the lemma ("by the Enlarging Lemma it follows that we can obtain su(n − 1)"), and it covers d = 2
--   - reading: "the subspace of H that does not contain |i⟩" → the orthogonal complement of |i⟩; reason: the only (d − 1)-dimensional subspace singled out by |i⟩ on which su(d − 1) is meant
--   - reading: "|j⟩ is another state in H" → |j⟩ linearly independent of |i⟩; reason: otherwise span{|i⟩, |j⟩} is one-dimensional and su(2) cannot act on it; span{|i⟩, |j⟩} is the same as for the component of |j⟩ orthogonal to |i⟩, which the proof uses
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Appendix D.2, Enlarging Lemma, p.256 (PDF p.273), proof to p.257 (PDF p.274)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_suAlg
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_suOn

open scoped Matrix

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

namespace BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv

/-- **Enlarging Lemma (§D.2), with the commutator clause corrected.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, Appendix D.2, "Enlarging Lemma", p.256 (PDF p.273), proof to p.257 (PDF p.274).

Let `H = ℂ^ι` have dimension `d`, let `|i⟩ ≠ 0` and let `|j⟩` be a vector with `|i⟩, |j⟩`
linearly independent. Let `H₁, H₂` be sets of Hamiltonians (represented as anti-Hermitian `iH`)
such that the Lie algebra generated by `H₁` contains `su(d − 1)` on the orthogonal complement of
`|i⟩` (acting as zero on `|i⟩`), and the Lie algebra generated by `H₂` contains `su(2)` on
`span{|i⟩, |j⟩}` (zero outside). Then
1. the Lie algebra generated by `H₁ ∪ H₂` contains `su(d)`;
2. if `d ≥ 3`, the commutators `[A, B]` with `A` in the Lie algebra generated by `H₁` and `B` in
   the Lie algebra generated by `H₂` generate exactly `su(d)` as a Lie algebra.

**Formalization Note.** `su(W)` extended by zero is `suOn`; the orthogonal complement of `|i⟩` is
`{w | ⟨i, w⟩ = 0}`. Lie algebras are real Lie spans in `Matrix ι ι ℂ`. The book's "[H₁, H₂] (all
commutators)" is read as commutators of elements of the generated algebras, which is how its proof
uses it (`X_jk ∈ H₁`, `X_ij ∈ H₂`); with literal generating sets it fails (see the mission notes),
as it does for `d = 2`. -/
theorem enlarging_lemma {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
    (vi vj : ι → ℂ) (hij : LinearIndependent ℂ ![vi, vj])
    (H₁ H₂ : Set (Matrix ι ι ℂ)) (hH₁ : ∀ A ∈ H₁, Aᴴ = -A) (hH₂ : ∀ A ∈ H₂, Aᴴ = -A)
    (h₁ : suOn {w | star vi ⬝ᵥ w = 0} ⊆
      (LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ) H₁ : Set (Matrix ι ι ℂ)))
    (h₂ : suOn {vi, vj} ⊆ (LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ) H₂ : Set (Matrix ι ι ℂ))) :
    suAlg ι ⊆ (LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ) (H₁ ∪ H₂) : Set (Matrix ι ι ℂ)) ∧
    (2 < Fintype.card ι →
      (LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ)
          {C | ∃ A ∈ LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ) H₁,
              ∃ B ∈ LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ) H₂, C = ⁅A, B⁆} :
        Set (Matrix ι ι ℂ)) = suAlg ι) := by sorry

end BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv
